      { 
        "type": "ObjectEvent", #(Here we are observing the condition of one specific product)
        "eventTime": "2026-08-17T16:00:00+01:00", #(The sensor reporting event was recorded at 4:00PM local time.
        "eventTimeZoneOffset": "+01:00",
        "epcList": [
          "urn:epc:id:sgtin:4012345.011111.9876" #(Product Serial 9876 is the object connected to this sensor event)
        ],
        "action": "OBSERVE", #(Nothing is being packed or transformed. The system is recording what it detected)
        "bizStep": "sensor_reporting", #(Osea the purpose of this event is recording what it detected)
        "readPoint": { 
          "id": "urn:epc:id:sgln:4012345.00005.0" #(This identifies the location where the sensor event was captured)
        },
        "sensorElementList": [ #(Here is where the sensor exceptions connected to the product begin)
          {
            "sensorReport": [
              {
                "exception": "gs1:ERROR_CONDITION", #(The system flagged an error condition)
                "uriValue": "https://example.com/ErrorCode-A827"
              }, #(Error code A827 points to additional information about the detected problem)
              {
                "exception": "gs1:ALARM_CONDITION", #(The system also recorded an alarm condition)
                "booleanValue": true #(Alarm was active when the event was captured) 
                
                #(Now we have the full picture. Product 9876 was being monitored at a specific 
                location when the sensor system recorded error code A827 and an active alarm. 
                 The event gives the business a traceable exception tied to a specific product, place, and time)            
            ]
          }      So what? Well instead of discovering the problem later, the business can query
                or subscribe to these alarm events and respond with a quality hold, investigation,
                or quick maintenance action.
       
            
            
      
      ##Jacobs Notes
      ##(Sensor Measurement divided into directional components.
        Imagine a quality manager responsible for a fleet of helicopters undergoing
        inspection and test flights. 
        
        One helicopter behaves unexpectedly during a test flight. However the engineering team 
        needs more than the pilots description. They need movement data connected to the exact 
        aircraft, testing location, and time. How can the business create a traceable record showing 
        how a specific helicopter was moving in multiple directions?? Let's take a look)
        "type": "ObjectEvent",
      ##(This event records an observation involving one specific serialized object.
        In our scenario, we are observing one helicopter. Nothing is being manufactured)
        "eventTime": "2026-08-25T15:00:00+01:00",
      ##(The movement observation occured on August 25, 2026 at 3pm local time)
        "eventTimeZoneOffset": "+01:00",
      ##(The local time was one hour ahead of UTC)
        "epcList": [
          "urn:epc:id:sgtin:4012345.022222.1267"
      ##(This identifies the exact serialized object being monitored. For our scenario
        serial 1267 identifies the helicopter undergoing the test flight)
        ],
        "action": "OBSERVE",
      ##(The system is recording what was detected. It is not changing the helicopter
        or adding to it.
        "bizStep": "sensor_reporting",
      ##(The purpose of this event is to report sensor info associated with the
        serialized object)
        "readPoint": {
          "id": "urn:epc:id:sgln:4012345.00005.0"
      ##(This identifies the read point where the EPCIS event was captured. In our scenario,
        a designated testing location withing an aviation facility.
        },
        "sensorElementList": [
          {
            "sensorReport": [
              {
                "type": "Speed",
        ##(The sensor report contains speed measurements. These measurements describe the
           movement of the entire helicopter, not the movement of its rotor blades)
                "value": 14.5,
                "component": "example:x",
                "uom": "MTS"
        ##(The speed recorded along the X component is 14.5 metres per second)
              },
              {
                "type": "Speed",
                "value": 12.8,
                "component": "example:y",
                "uom": "MTS"
        ##(The speed recorded along the Y component is 12.8 metres per second) 
              },
              {
                "type": "Speed",
                "value": 100,
                "component": "example:z",
                "uom": "MTS"
        ##(The speed recorded along the Z component is 100 metres per second at that moment)
        ##(Now we connect the story. Helicopter 1267 was observed on August 25, 2026 at 3 pm at
          read point 4012345.00005.0. The event records the helicopters speed across the three directional components) 
          X: 14.5 m/s
          Y: 12.8 m/s
          Z: 100 m/s
                
        ##(Now the quality manager investigating unexpected movement during a test flight can connect the 
           measurements to the exact helicopter, observation time, and testing location.

           So what? Whale instead of relying on pilot recollection, emails, or disconnected logs, the engineering
           team has movement data connected to the aircrafts traceability history. 
          
           Across repeated test flights, this info could help organizations compare movement patterns, investigate 
           irregularities, and identify helicopters that may require additional inspection.

           This could enable faster investigations, stronger quality management, better audit evidence, and
           less aircraft downtime)

        

      
              }
            ]
          }
        ] ##Jacobs Notes
