          ##(Asset Visibility Scenario - Jacobs Notes)
          Imagine a governance manager responsible for thousands of reusable assets like company vehicles,
          machinery, crates, containers, or kegs moving between warehouses, carriers, and customers.

          With so many assets moving across different locations, 
          How can the business consistently identify where each asset was last observed?

          Let’s look at how JSON & EPCIS 2.0 can connect a specific asset to its location,
          time, and sensor data within a traceable asset management record.
      {
        "type": "ObjectEvent", 
        ##(This event records an observation involving one specific asset, returnable asset
        identified by a GRAI using Asset Identifier 8003)
        ##(AI 8003 tells the system that 952012200000001004 is a GRAI)
        "eventTime": "2026-08-24T11:00:00+01:00",
        ##(So we see an asset observation event occured on August, 24, 2026 at 11AM local time)
        "eventTimeZoneOffset": "+01:00",
        ##(This line describes how the local time was one hour ahead of UTC)
        ##(JSON is painting asset journey story)
        "epcList": [
          "https://id.example.com/8003/952012200000001004"
        ##(This identifies the specfic asset being observed. Application Identifier 8003
          represents a Global Returnable Asset Identifier GRAI, which is used to identify returnable asset.

          For our scenario, lets say this identifier belongs to one reusable keg.)
        ],
        "action": "OBSERVE",
        ##(The system is recording information about the asset. It is not creating the asset or 
        changing its identity)
        "bizStep": "https://ref.gs1.org/cbv/BizStep-sensor_reporting",
        ##(A sensor is reporting information associated with the asset)
        "readPoint": {
          "id": "https://id.example.com/414/4012345000054"
          ##(This identifies the read point where the EPCIS event was captured. Its important to 
          note the read point and the reported coordinates serve different purposes. The read point
          identifies the business capture point, while the sensor report provide the assets geographic location)
        },
        "sensorElementList": [
          {
            "sensorReport": [
              {
                "type": "Angle", ##(lat and long are angular coordinates/attributes)
                "coordinateReferenceSystem": "http://www.opengis.net/def/crs/OGC/1.3/CRS84",
          ##(CRS84 provides standardized mapping rules so different
           systems interpret and plot the coordinates consistently.)
                "value": 42.698334,
                "component": "cbv:Comp-latitude",
                "uom": "DD"
          ##(The assets latitude was recorded as 42.698334 decimal degrees)
              },
              {
                "type": "Angle",
                "coordinateReferenceSystem": "http://www.opengis.net/def/crs/OGC/1.3/CRS84",
                "value": 23.319941,
                "component": "cbv:Comp-longitude",
                "uom": "DD"
          ##(The asset's longitude was recorded as 23.319941)
              }
            ]
          }
          ##(Now we connect the story
          Returnable asset 952012200000001004 was observed on August 24, 2028 at 11am local time. The event
          connects the asset's GRAI to read point 4012345000054 at the following coordinates:
          
          Latitude: 42.698334°
          Longitude: 23.319941°
          
          In our scenario, a data governance manager investigating a missing keg can use this event
          as its last known recorded location instead of beginning with phone calls.)

          ## So what? 
          Whale instead of spending hours contacting warehouses, carriers,
          and customers without a clear starting point, the business can begin
          its investigation with the asset's last recorded location.

          This helps the manager narrow the search, identify the appropriate
          facility or trading partner, and recover the asset faster.

          Across thousands of reusable assets, these location events could also
          help identify repeated loss patterns, and assets that
          are sitting idle instead of supporting operations.

          Bottom line that visibility can help the business avoid unnecessary costs.

          The value is not simply knowing where one asset was observed. It is creating
          a repeatable and traceable process for managing an entire network of returnable
          assets. Vehicles, Machinery, Industrial Contrainers, etc.

        ]
